#%PAM-1.0

# greetd's own stack, plus the gnome-keyring lines SDDM used to provide.
#
# Without these the login keyring stays locked after boot: greetd authenticates
# through PAM but nothing hands the password to gnome-keyring, so ~/.local/
# share/keyrings/login.keyring is never unlocked and anything reading secrets
# (nextcloud-calendar.py's secret-tool lookup, nm-applet, ssh keys) comes up
# empty. /etc/pam.d/sddm carried the same three lines.
#
# Every added line is `optional` and prefixed with `-`, so a missing or failing
# pam_gnome_keyring can never block a login. If this file is ever wrong anyway,
# a TTY (ctrl+alt+F1) authenticates through /etc/pam.d/login and is unaffected.
#
# The `password` line SDDM had is deliberately omitted: greetd has no password
# stack to hang it off, and `passwd` rekeys the keyring through /etc/pam.d/passwd
# regardless of which display manager is installed.
#
# A finger logs in too, without a typed password ever waiting on the reader:
# greetd runs one conversation, and cannot cancel one while pam_fprintd scans.
# So the first question is the password's, as always, and only an empty answer
# (the greeter sends one by itself as it opens, and for Enter on an empty field,
# where greeter-env.py found a reader) goes to pam_fprintd: finger-asked succeeds for an empty answer
# alone, and any other result skips the reader's lines. An empty answer never
# reaches the password check, or pam_faillock would count each finger as a
# wrong password. Fail-safe both ways: without finger-asked the password path
# is as before, and without pam_fprintd an empty answer fails.
#
# A finger gets three tries, each a pam_fprintd line of its own with one try:
# only so is every finger that did not match counted (PAM_MAXTRIES, which
# pam_faillock's authfail notes), whatever follows it. One line with three
# tries told a third miss only, and two misses then a timeout nothing, as
# often as anyone liked. The count is the fingers' own
# (dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger, kept across a reboot, which the login
# screen is one power button away from): after five misses in a quarter of
# an hour the reader is not asked for ten minutes (faillock.conf's
# fail_interval and unlock_time), and the stack says so. The password's
# count, and so the password, is untouched by it; a finger that matches
# clears the fingers' count (authsucc), and so does the password, even while
# the reader is not asked: the account line clears it then, which pam_faillock
# does only after an auth line of its own ran, hence the optional authsucc
# after the password's check. A reader that times
# out, has no print for the user or is gone fails the login uncounted (die):
# the greeter's own scan times out whenever nobody is there.
# The reader waits 5 s for a finger: greetd answers nothing else meanwhile, so
# a password entered then waits that long at most (the greeter sends it after).
# pam_gnome_keyring gets no password from a finger; the login keyring is kept
# unencrypted for that on an encrypted disk (Jupiter-installer's phase 4). There
# it gets no password from a typed one either: gnome-keyring opens a plain
# keyring with the password it is given and saves it encrypted with that
# password, which a finger then cannot open. disk-encrypted (run as root,
# installed from root's copy) skips the password's line where / is on LUKS; the
# session's line still starts the daemon. On an unencrypted disk the keyring is
# encrypted with the login password, which a typed one opens as before.
#
# This file is in pacman's backup set, so an upgrade of greetd leaves it alone
# and drops /etc/pam.d/greetd.pacnew next to it. Diff that after a greetd update.

auth       required     pam_securetty.so
auth       requisite    pam_nologin.so
auth       [success=ok default=10]    pam_exec.so quiet expose_authtok /etc/greetd/finger-asked
auth       [success=2 default=ignore] pam_faillock.so preauth silent dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       [default=ignore]           pam_echo.so Fingerprint not recognised: type the password
auth       requisite    pam_deny.so
auth       [success=5 maxtries=ignore default=die] pam_fprintd.so max-tries=1 timeout=5
auth       [ignore=ignore default=die] pam_faillock.so authfail dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       [success=3 maxtries=ignore default=die] pam_fprintd.so max-tries=1 timeout=5
auth       [ignore=ignore default=die] pam_faillock.so authfail dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       [success=1 maxtries=bad default=die]    pam_fprintd.so max-tries=1 timeout=5
auth       [default=die]              pam_faillock.so authfail dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       [success=done default=die] pam_faillock.so authsucc dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       include      system-local-login
-auth      optional     pam_faillock.so authsucc dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5
auth       [success=1 default=ignore] pam_exec.so quiet /etc/greetd/disk-encrypted
-auth      optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so

account    include      system-local-login
-account   optional     pam_faillock.so dir=/var/lib/faillock-finger deny=5

session    include      system-local-login
-session   optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so    auto_start
